\section*{1. Introduction}

Typological knowledge bases, such as the typological database (TCC) (Heine et al., 2017), document linguistic features across numerous languages. These databases are frequently employed in NLP applications, including language modeling, word embedding, and machine translation. However, a significant limitation is the scarcity of data for many languages, which results in incomplete entries. To address this, we propose a method to automatically classify languages into specific TCC types using only text data. Our approach utilizes a feature selection technique to identify the most relevant linguistic features for classification. We evaluate the method's effectiveness by classifying languages into one of the 10 TCC types. Our results indicate that the selected features yield an F1 score of 0.84. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the most important features for classification are consistent with the linguistic reasoning underlying the TCC. This research enables the automatic classification of languages, thereby facilitating the expansion of typological knowledge bases.

