\section*{1. Introduction}

Smartphones are now ubiquitous, and their advanced positioning capabilities have enabled a wide range of location-based services (LBS) that rely on indoor positioning systems (IPS). Despite these advancements, IPS remain unreliable, frequently yielding inaccurate location estimates. Consequently, there is a critical need to enhance IPS accuracy to support LBS applications. This paper presents a method for predicting human mobility patterns to improve IPS performance. By employing a Kalman filter, we estimate the most likely position of a user at any given time, utilizing both the current IPS location estimate and the user's historical mobility data. Our findings indicate that this approach significantly boosts localization accuracy. Furthermore, the proposed method is computationally efficient, making it suitable for real-time applications.

\section*{2. Related Work}

Recent research has explored various IPS techniques, including Wi-Fi fingerprinting, triangulation, and trilateration. While Wi-Fi fingerprinting is widely used, it suffers from high computational costs and requires extensive training data. Triangulation and trilateration offer lower computational demands but are hindered by the intermittent nature of wireless signals. Additionally, IPS often struggle to distinguish between different access points (APs) located in close proximity.

Human mobility patterns have been extensively studied, revealing that people tend to visit a limited number of locations frequently. Studies have shown that location prediction is highly accurate when utilizing historical mobility data. Furthermore, it has been observed that mobility patterns are relatively consistent across different days. These insights have been leveraged to improve IPS accuracy.

