\textbf{1. Introduction}
Recent breakthroughs in deep learning have driven substantial progress in video prediction and generation. However, a critical challenge persists: the difficulty in effectively modeling long-term temporal dynamics. In this work, we present a novel method called the "video game engine," which emulates the internal processes of a game engine to predict future video frames. By modeling static and long-term dynamic factors in distinct ways, our approach achieves superior performance. The core idea involves two modules that simulate the physics and graphics engines, respectively, to model dynamic and static factors. Additionally, a "rendering" module is introduced to produce the final predicted frames. Our method successfully captures long-term temporal dynamics, and experiments confirm that it outperforms state-of-the-art techniques for both video prediction and generation.
